    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a139cfb4_02904f53",
        "filename": "javalib/src/android/system/virtualmachine/VirtualMachineConfig.java",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 431,
      "author": {
        "id": 1132673
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-09-06T15:38:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I am not sure if we want to make this available to apps. This might be too powerful to be provided to apps. WDYT?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 426,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 431,
        "endChar": 9
      },
      "revId": "2704543c167c5d1e5e266207d9d7b6373e478bfa",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e3ec908c_9bc225db",
        "filename": "javalib/src/android/system/virtualmachine/VirtualMachineConfig.java",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 431,
      "author": {
        "id": 1060831
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-09-06T16:27:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Well. We are now using this in some of our benchmark tests, and it would be nice if we built those against the system API. And if we want to disallow priv apps from using this we\u0027d need to put this behind the custom VM permission, but we can then grant that to the test app.\n\nIf crosvm were running in the app\u0027s cgroup, then the set of CPUs available to it would be controlled by Android. (E.g. I think a background app only gets at most one CPU.)\n\nBut it isn\u0027t so you\u0027re probably right.",
      "parentUuid": "a139cfb4_02904f53",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 426,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 431,
        "endChar": 9
      },
      "revId": "2704543c167c5d1e5e266207d9d7b6373e478bfa",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "778b8792_986a71bf",
        "filename": "javalib/src/android/system/virtualmachine/VirtualMachineConfig.java",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 431,
      "author": {
        "id": 1132673
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-09-06T16:45:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "For ordinary apps, we definitely want that this is uncontrollable (i.e. inherited from its parent). For platform\u0027s internal use (e.g. compos), we want to have control, but they don\u0027t need to use this Java API. So, I don\u0027t think this API is quite useful modulo tests. What about dropping @SystemApi here and also enforcing that overriding cpu affinity needs a privileged permission? (the latter could be done as a separate change) Tests could be built with private APIs.",
      "parentUuid": "e3ec908c_9bc225db",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 426,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 431,
        "endChar": 9
      },
      "revId": "2704543c167c5d1e5e266207d9d7b6373e478bfa",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0b543149_fd90ddeb",
        "filename": "javalib/src/android/system/virtualmachine/VirtualMachineConfig.java",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 431,
      "author": {
        "id": 1060831
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-09-07T10:14:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I agree with all of that except this\n\n\u003e Tests could be built with private APIs.\nWe don\u0027t have any other clients of the API at the moment. And it really seems to me that the tests should be testing the APIs we are exposing.\n\nOTOH the only place we need this at the moment is in the benchmarks. Perhaps we could handle those differently - building just those against the platform API, or accessing via reflection + adding a new permission?\n\n\u003e enforcing that overriding cpu affinity needs a privileged permission\nCalling the API at all requires a privileged permission. Would we want to keep CPU affinity as requiring platform signature? Probably, if only from a system health perspective.\n\nI guess CPU affinity is currently restricted via cgroups? Can we check whether the caller would be able to do this themselves?\n\nHow does crosvm implement this thread affinity anyway?",
      "parentUuid": "778b8792_986a71bf",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 426,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 431,
        "endChar": 9
      },
      "revId": "2704543c167c5d1e5e266207d9d7b6373e478bfa",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "bd8d8fba_29adb25f",
        "filename": "javalib/src/android/system/virtualmachine/VirtualMachineConfig.java",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 431,
      "author": {
        "id": 1132673
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-09-07T13:03:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We could annotate this as @Test, if we don\u0027t like building the tests against platform APIs. \n\n\u003e Calling the API at all requires a privileged permission.\n\nWe can\u0027t, because this API doesn\u0027t invokve any IPC to the service. The check can happen only after the vm config parcel having this information is sent to VS.\n\n\u003e I guess CPU affinity is currently restricted via cgroups?\n\nYes, that\u0027s my understanding.\n\n\u003e How does crosvm implement this thread affinity anyway?\n\nUsing set_schedaffinity. https://cs.android.com/android/platform/superproject/+/master:external/crosvm/src/crosvm/sys/unix/vcpu.rs;drc\u003da6af06cb700f0b2bd17ae36cbc8835aa3e2c8eac;l\u003d125",
      "parentUuid": "0b543149_fd90ddeb",
